  "title": "CHAPTER 2: GEOGRAPHY OF UTTARAKHAND",
  "order": 2,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "uyk8ts4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2.1 Location and Extent"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Uttarakhand's topography spans from the outer Shivalik ranges to the Greater Himalayas.",
        "The Panch Prayag consists of five sacred river confluences located along the course of the Alaknanda River.",
        "Major peaks like Nanda Devi, Kamet, and Trisul define the northern mountain boundary of the state.",
        "Strategic mountain passes such as Mana, Niti, and Lipulekh connect Uttarakhand with the Tibetan Plateau.",
        "Major river systems originating from regional glaciers like Gangotri and Yamunotri serve as key perennial water sources for North India."
      ],
      "id": "c774zh9"
    },
    {
