          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Terai</p>",
            "<p>Fertile, flat plains south of Bhabar; dense forests formerly; now agricultural</p>",
            "<p>200–300 m</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shiwalik Hills</p>",
            "<p>Outer Himalayas; lowest ranges; alluvial valleys called 'Duns'</p>",
            "<p>600–1500 m</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Lesser Himalayas / Mahabharat</p>",
            "<p>Middle ranges; most habitation; Mussoorie, Lansdowne ridges</p>",
            "<p>1500–4500 m</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Greater Himalayas</p>",
            "<p>Main Himalayan range; glaciers, high peaks, trans-Himalayan valleys</p>",
            "<p>4500–7817 m</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Trans-Himalayan / Inner Himalaya</p>",
            "<p>Beyond main range; Milam, Johar valleys; Tibetan plateau edge</p>",
            "<p>&gt;5500 m</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u0f8w5n",
      "content": "Important 'Duns' (intermontane valleys): Dehradun Dun (most famous), Kotah Dun, Patli Dun (Yamuna valley), Chandpur Garhi Dun. These duns were formed by tectonic movements and are agriculturally productive."
    },
